-- scripts/dst/discovery_dst.ail
--
-- WI-A13 stage 2: discovery against `driver_only`. What the REAL driver
-- requested, recorded into D2's interaction log, and graded in both directions
-- against witnesses the recorder did not write.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT THIS ASSERTS, AND WHY EACH AXIS IS HERE
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
--   A. COMPLETENESS (under-recording). Every request the driver made is in the
--      log. Named by the parent handoff; the failure it catches is a recorder
--      that drops a class, which produces a consistent, reproducible,
--      replayable log describing a shorter run than the one that happened.
--
--   B. FIDELITY (over-recording). Every interaction in the log corresponds to a
--      request the driver made. NOT named by any handoff — it is the mirror
--      image cluster 7's S1 result says must be named, because a one-sided
--      assertion set cannot see failures in the other direction. Its failure is
--      a log describing a LONGER run than happened, and strict replay reports
--      the surplus as an "unused interaction" — a HarnessFailure that names the
--      recorder's defect as if it were the system's.
--
--      A and B are the two arms of ONE comparison (`class_balance`), so neither
--      can be deleted without the other staring back.
--
--   C. THE ASSERTION IS NOT VACUOUS. The same scenario is run through the
--      NON-recording adapters and must come out RED. This is the executable
--      form of "written before the recorder, proven red against a driver that
--      records nothing", and keeping it in the suite is what stops a future
--      refactor from turning the checker into a tautology.
--
--   D. MUTATION (cluster 5's C5). Every guard is demonstrated going red under a
--      deliberate mutation of the REAL recorded log — a dropped class, an
--      invented interaction, a shifted ordinal, an unaccounted clock advance.
--      A guard that never fires is the defect these items exist to prevent.
--
--   E. NEGATIVE CONTROL (cluster 7's S1). The real, unmutated log must SURVIVE.
--      A suite of only-failing fixtures cannot see an over-strict checker, and
--      cluster 7 measured that being the difference between a correct and a
--      silently wrong reading of D2. Here the negative control is axis A+B
--      itself passing on the real run — which is why it is stated as its own
--      named check rather than left implicit.
--
--   F. DETERMINISM, last and least. Two runs of the same world produce the same
--      log. It is required by D7 and it has caught none of the fifteen silent
--      defects this project has found, for the structural reason that a
--      recorder which drops a class drops it identically twice.
--
--   G. THE LOG IS A VALID PROGRAM. The recorded interactions, wrapped in
--      `driver_only`'s real manifest, pass stage 1's pure structural validator.
--      This is the join between the two stages: a recorder that produces
--      something the decoder rejects has produced an unreplayable artifact, and
--      the two were written far enough apart to disagree.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE WITNESSES, AND WHY THEY ARE AN ORACLE
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- The load-bearing property is TWO AUTHORS, TWO RECORDS, ONE EXECUTION. The
-- interaction log is written by the world adapters in `src/core/ports.ail`. The
-- witnesses are written by production driver code that knows nothing about the
-- recorder:
--
--   provider  `ProviderCallPrepared`, appended to the RETURNED ledger trace by
--             session.ail:1997 and emitted separately from the `dispatch_step`
--             call at :2008. Cross-component, and it exists whether or not the
--             port recorded anything.
--   tool      the tool-result messages the driver returned, built by
--             `tool_phase.ail`'s `tool_outcome_message`. The STRONGER form of
--             this witness is the wire's `v2_tool_dispatch_start`
--             (tool_phase.ail:332), emitted BEFORE the port calls at :342-350 —
--             but it goes to the wire through `ledger_emit` and is never
--             appended to the returned trace, so it cannot be read from inside
--             this script. `make discovery` compares it against the census line
--             printed below, which is where that witness is actually consumed.
--   approval  the world's own `approvals` cursor delta — the response-producing
--             mechanism, not the log-writing one.
--   clock     the world's own `clock_ms` delta, checked EXACTLY against the sum
--             of recorded advances. General across classes: every class may
--             advance time, so no single class's recorder can satisfy it alone.
--   env       source-derived, and of a different kind. See `dst_discovery`.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE SCENARIOS
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- Two, and the pair matters more than either alone. Both send every tool call
-- to the approval channel; they differ in what the operator answers.
--
--   approve  three approvals, all granted, so three native tool dispatches
--            follow. approvals = 3, tools = 3.
--   deny     three approvals, all refused, so NO tool dispatch happens at all.
--            approvals = 3, tools = 0.
--
-- `deny` earns its place, and the reason was MEASURED rather than assumed. The
-- first version of this header claimed it was needed to catch a recorder that
-- logs the approval read as a tool interaction; that claim is false, and the
-- mutation says so — the approval balance catches that one in BOTH scenarios.
-- What `deny` uniquely catches is a miswiring of the CHECKER ITSELF:
--
--   grade the tool balance against `approvals_consumed` instead of
--   `tool_dispatches` — a one-line confusion between two adjacent witness
--   fields — and `approve` PASSES, because both witnesses are 3 there. `deny`
--   fails immediately, because the tool witness is 0 and the approval witness
--   is 3.
--
-- So `deny` is the negative control for the ASSERTION, not only for the
-- recorder: it is the only scenario in which the tool class's expected count is
-- zero, and the only one in which two witnesses that happen to agree are forced
-- apart. Verified by deliberately introducing that miswiring and observing
-- exactly this split.

